# Alchemy beta.79 + Effect Worker implementation asset

## Decision

Build `apps/mischief` as one Effect-native `Cloudflare.Worker` whose construction phase builds one `HttpRouter` handler. Register the root routes and `McpServer.layerHttp({ path: "/mcp" })` in that router, then call `HttpRouter.toHttpEffect` once when the isolate boots.

Adopt the existing `ratstack.sh` zone explicitly. Put the apex on the Worker's `domain.name` and `www.ratstack.sh` in `domain.redirects`. Do not also declare apex or `www` DNS records: Alchemy's custom-domain support manages their DNS and certificates. Keep MCP at `https://ratstack.sh/mcp`; that path needs no DNS record.

Add worker-safe package subpaths before importing `@rat-stack/capability` in `mischief`. The current root barrel retains part of the Node subprocess sandbox and roughly doubles the projection bundle even when only `toToolkit` is selected.

## Evidence boundary

This asset uses only local source:

- Alchemy mirror docs and examples under `.agent_sources/github.com/alchemy-run/alchemy/`.
- The installed `alchemy@2.0.0-beta.79` package under `node_modules/.pnpm/alchemy@2.0.0-beta.79_*/node_modules/alchemy/`.
- Effect rc.116 source under `.agent_sources/github.com/Effect-TS/effect/`.
- Current rat-stack source and built packages.
- Scratch bundles under `/tmp/ratstack-bundle-research-20260921-worker/`.

No cloud plan, deploy, or live workerd process was run.

## Copy-ready Worker shape

This uses the class form exercised by Alchemy's Effect Worker example. `fetch` is an Effect requiring the request service and returning an `HttpServerResponse`; the Alchemy bridge adapts it to the Cloudflare Worker surface.

```ts
// apps/mischief/src/worker.ts
import * as Cloudflare from "alchemy/Cloudflare";
import * as Effect from "effect/Effect";
import * as Layer from "effect/Layer";
import { McpProtocol, McpServer } from "effect/unstable/ai";
import { HttpRouter, HttpServerResponse } from "effect/unstable/http";
import { toToolkit } from "@rat-stack/capability/toolkit";

import { capabilities } from "./capabilities.js";

const tools = toToolkit(capabilities);

const mcpTransport = McpServer.layerHttp({
  name: "rat-stack",
  version: "0.1.0",
  description: "Learn and use the rat-stack template",
  websiteUrl: "https://ratstack.sh/",
  path: "/mcp",
  protocols: [
    McpProtocol.v2025_06_18,
    McpProtocol.v2025_03_26,
    McpProtocol.v2024_11_05,
  ],
  allowedOrigins: ["https://ratstack.sh", "http://localhost:1337"],
});

const mcp = McpServer.toolkit(tools.toolkit).pipe(
  Layer.provideMerge(tools.layer),
  Layer.provide(mcpTransport),
);

export const routes = Layer.mergeAll(
  HttpRouter.add(
    "GET",
    "/",
    HttpServerResponse.text("rat-stack\n", {
      contentType: "text/plain; charset=utf-8",
    }),
  ),
  mcp,
);

export default class Mischief extends Cloudflare.Worker<Mischief>()(
  "Mischief",
  {
    main: import.meta.url,
    dev: { port: 1337 },
    domain: {
      name: "ratstack.sh",
      redirects: ["www.ratstack.sh"],
      zoneName: "ratstack.sh",
    },
    workersDev: false,
  },
  Effect.gen(function* makeMischief() {
    return {
      fetch: yield* HttpRouter.toHttpEffect(routes),
    };
  }),
) {}
```

`McpServer.layerHttp` registers GET/PUT/PATCH/DELETE/OPTIONS and POST behavior at one streamable-HTTP path. It requires an `HttpRouter`; `HttpRouter.toHttpEffect` creates that router, builds the layer stack once, and returns the per-request HTTP Effect. Origin-less MCP clients remain valid; requests with an `Origin` header must exactly match `allowedOrigins`.

The root response above is only the smallest wiring example. The implementation step can replace it with content negotiation and merge in resource/prompt registration layers without changing the Worker bridge.

**Sources**

